    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d226">

                <pb facs="0662" n="540" sameAs="frus:frus1889/pg540"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Hubbard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-03-15"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 15,
                        1889</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 291.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your interesting dispatch No. 547 of the
                    14th ultimo, in relation to the promulgation of the new Japanese constitution on
                    the 11th of February last, has been received and the accompanying papers have
                    been filed as part of the history of this memorable event.</p>
                <p>The people of the New World, who have ever testified a lively interest in the
                    welfare and progress of Japan, can not but view in this formal adoption of a
                    scheme of constitutional government a bright augury for the future of a country
                    and a people bound to them by so many ties of continued friendship and intimate
                    intercourse. That this measure will secure the liberties and promote the
                    happiness of the Japanese people and tend to secure for their empire the
                    autonomous position which rightfully belongs to it in the concourse of nations
                    can not be doubted.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Blaine.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
